"A capillary is thinner than a single hair.": "2a87e2827b64",
"After the race, my pulse was 150 beats a minute.": "4d186764bb98",
"Blood travels through three kinds of tubes. Arteries carry it away from the heart. Their walls are thick and stretchy, and the pulse you feel is an artery swelling with each squeeze. Arteries branch into capillaries, tubes so thin that blood cells must line up in single file to pass through. Their walls are only one cell thick, so oxygen and food can slip out and waste can slip in. Capillaries join into veins, which carry the blood back to the heart. For a long time, people in Europe believed that the body used up blood and kept making new blood. In 1628, the English doctor William Harvey showed that it must flow around and around in a circle.": "6a5e8d28ed38",
"Each pump has two rooms, called chambers. The upper chamber, the atrium, collects blood. The lower chamber, the ventricle, is the strong one that squeezes it out. Between the chambers, and at the exits, are valves, flaps that work like one-way doors. When they snap shut, they make the sound a doctor hears through a stethoscope: lub-dub, lub-dub. The first sound is the doors between the atria and the ventricles closing, and the second is the doors at the exits closing.": "2dc2e1402ab1",
