"Fish take oxygen from the water with their gills.": "719a1ba4259d",
"Press two fingers gently on the thumb side of your wrist. Each little bump you feel is your pulse, and every bump is one squeeze of your heart. Your heart is a muscle about the size of your fist, sitting behind your breastbone between your two lungs. It beats about 100,000 times a day, and in a long life, close to three billion times, without ever stopping to rest.": "9218a26ccc3d",
"Press two fingers gently on the thumb side of your wrist. Each little bump you feel is your pulse, and every bump is one squeeze of your heart. Your heart is a muscle about the size of your fist, sitting behind your breastbone between your two lungs. It beats about 100,000 times a day, and in a long life, close to three billion times, without ever stopping to rest. Why does it work so hard? Every cell in your body needs a steady supply of oxygen and food, and every cell makes waste, such as carbon dioxide, that has to be carried away. Blood is the delivery service. An adult has about five liters of it, and the heart pumps all of it around the body roughly once every minute when you are resting. The surprising part is that your heart is really two pumps, side by side, divided by a wall of muscle. The right side collects blood coming back from the body, which has given away much of its oxygen, and pumps it to the lungs. There the blood drops off carbon dioxide and picks up fresh oxygen. The left side collects that oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it out to the rest of the body. Both sides squeeze at the same moment, so every heartbeat sends blood around both loops at once. The left side has the thickest wall, because it has to push blood all the way to your toes and back. Each pump has two rooms, called chambers. The upper chamber, the atrium, collects blood. The lower chamber, the ventricle, is the strong one that squeezes it out. Between the chambers, and at the exits, are valves, flaps that work like one-way doors. When they snap shut, they make the sound a doctor hears through a stethoscope: lub-dub, lub-dub. The first sound is the doors between the atria and the ventricles closing, and the second is the doors at the exits closing. Blood travels through three kinds of tubes. Arteries carry it away from the heart. Their walls are thick and stretchy, and the pulse you feel is an artery swelling with each squeeze. Arteries branch into capillaries, tubes so thin that blood cells must line up in single file to pass through. Their walls are only one cell thick, so oxygen and food can slip out and waste can slip in. Capillaries join into veins, which carry the blood back to the heart. For a long time, people in Europe believed that the body used up blood and kept making new blood. In 1628, the English doctor William Harvey showed that it must flow around and around in a circle. Your heart rate changes all day. It is slowest when you are asleep, and it speeds up when you run, get excited, or feel scared, so that more oxygen reaches your working muscles. For most adults, a resting pulse is between 60 and 100 beats a minute, and children's hearts usually beat a little faster. Regular exercise makes the heart muscle stronger, so it can pump more blood with each beat. Now find your own pulse, count it for 15 seconds, and let the heart in the model beat with yours.": "7f7656ec7569",
